    "key_insight": "The simplicity premium is a MYTH in our paper trading data. Complex strategies (avg Sharpe 2.51) beat simple ones (avg 2.03). The real story: (1) turnover correlates with HIGHER Sharpe because active strategies capture more return, (2) regime-based strategies like piecewise/adaptive_tier achieve high Sharpe AND stability by going to cash in high-VIX, (3) the true winner is not simplest or most complex \u2014 it is the strategy with the best risk-adjusted design (piecewise_conservative: 2 params, Sharpe 3.16, MDD -2.5%).",
    "why_demiguel_doesnt_apply": "DeMiguel et al (2009) showed 1/N beats complex PORTFOLIO OPTIMIZATION (mean-variance, Bayes-Stein etc) because of estimation error in return forecasts. Our strategies are RULE-BASED VIX-driven allocation, not return-optimized. VIX-based rules have NO return estimation error \u2014 they scale exposure to observed volatility. This is why complexity works here: parameters are thresholds on observables (VIX), not estimates of future returns.",
